issues.body:
### What happened?


Chinese queries return no matches even when the tool metadata contains the same Chinese words.

The shared tokenizer in `packages/coding-agent/src/extensions/tool-search/tool.ts` splits on `/[^a-z0-9]+/`, which discards Chinese characters entirely. Both `tool_search` and codemode’s `searchTools()` use this tokenizer through `Bm25Ranker`.

This is reproducible directly against the published package, without loading any custom extensions.



### Steps to reproduce

With `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent@1.0.4` installed locally, run:

```sh
node --input-type=module <<'JS'
import {
  tokenize,
  Bm25Ranker,
} from './node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/extensions/tool-search/tool.js';

const documents = [
  { name: 'search_notes', text: '搜索记录' },
];

console.log(tokenize('记录'));
// Actual: []

console.log(new Bm25Ranker().rank('记录', documents, 8));
// Actual: []
JS
```

### Expected behavior

Chinese words should be preserved and segmented so that `记录` can match the tool description `搜索记录`.

This is about matching existing Chinese metadata, rather than translating Chinese queries into English. Existing English identifier splitting and stemming should continue to work.

I’m willing to contribute a small fix with regression tests for the tokenizer and both discovery paths. Would this approach be welcome?

### Version

1.0.4

`Conversation.submit({ type: "input" })` with no `content` is accepted. The input is stored as a user entry with no `content` (`{"role":"user","timestamp":...}`), and the run then settles `unanswered`, reason `faulted`, detail `content is not iterable`. The conversation now holds a malformed entry.

TypeScript rejects the call, but a JS caller, or a cast, gets no error at submit time. I hit it by resubmitting after a run failed on a provider 429, meaning "carry on".

Repro, `@earendil-works/pi-durable` 1.4.0, Node 22, provider `cloudflare-workers-ai` (any provider should do):

```ts
import { BACKGROUND_CONTEXT as context } from "@earendil-works/chord/context";
import { builtinModels } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/providers/all";
import { createRegistry, Harness, MemoryStorage } from "@earendil-works/pi-durable";

const harness = await Harness.open(new MemoryStorage(), { models: builtinModels(), registry: createRegistry() }, context);
const conv = await harness.createConversation({ ownership: { kind: "ownerless" }, agent: { model: { provider: "cloudflare-workers-ai", modelId: "@cf/google/gemma-4-26b-a4b-it" } } }, context);
console.log((await (await conv.submit({ type: "input", content: "Say hi." }, context)).wait(context)).status); // done
const bare = await (await conv.submit({ type: "input" } as never, context)).wait(context);
console.log(bare.status, bare.reason, bare.detail); // unanswered faulted content is not iterable
```

Expected: `submit()` throws a validation error and nothing is stored, or the input is stored with empty content. A related question: what is the supported way to continue a conversation whose last run ended in a provider error, without adding a new user message?

### What happened?

With stdin kept open, an idle `--mode rpc` process stays alive after an extension calls `ctx.shutdown()` asynchronously, for example from a timer callback. The shutdown request is not processed until another RPC command arrives.

The [RPC shutdown handler](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/f09d01b85e0384647fcab6779d35ab9dabe04be3/packages/coding-agent/src/modes/rpc/rpc-mode.ts#L345-L347) only sets `shutdownRequested`; it checks the flag after stdin commands or `agent_settled`. An idle request has neither trigger.

### Steps to reproduce

Save `shutdown-probe.mjs` in an empty directory:

```js
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("session_start", (_, ctx) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      process.stderr.write("shutdown requested\n");
      ctx.shutdown();
    }, 200);
  });
  pi.on("session_shutdown", () => process.stderr.write("session_shutdown\n"));
}
```

Run with stdin left open:

```sh
PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR="$(mktemp -d)" pi --mode rpc --no-session -ne -e ./shutdown-probe.mjs
```

After `shutdown requested`, the process remains alive and `session_shutdown` is not emitted. Enter `{"type":"get_state"}` followed by Enter: the query succeeds, `session_shutdown` is emitted, and Pi exits with code 0. Reproduced with isolated configuration and only the probe extension enabled.

### Expected behavior

An idle `ctx.shutdown()` request should perform orderly shutdown without requiring another stdin command.

### Version

Pi 1.4.0; Node.js 22.23.2; macOS 27.2 arm64. The same handler is present at commit `f09d01b8`.

_Disclosure: drafted by an AI agent (pi) from my session logs and the pi-ai source; reviewed and posted by me._

### What happened?

An extension reloaded mid-session and stopped registering some of its tools. After that, every request to `anthropic/claude-opus-5-5` fails:

```
400 invalid_request_error: messages.466.content.1: tool_addition/tool_removal
references unknown tool 'gmail_accounts' (error_code: tool_reference_unresolved)
```

The removal is saved in the session, so retries and new prompts all fail. The session can only be used again after setting `compat.supportsMidConvoToolChanges: false`.

The removed tools were in the first tool set, which goes into the top-level `tools` list. pi-ai then sends a `tool_removal` for them, and Anthropic seems to accept `tool_removal` only for tools added inline with `tool_addition`.

### Steps to reproduce

1. `pi -ne -e ./ext.ts --model anthropic/claude-opus-5-5 -p "Run 'echo one', then 'echo two', then 'echo three', then reply DONE."`
2. `ext.ts` registers a tool `foo` and removes it at the first `turn_end`:

```ts
import type { ExtensionAPI } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
import { Type } from "typebox";

export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {
	pi.registerTool({
		name: "foo", label: "foo", description: "Unused test tool.",
		parameters: Type.Object({}),
		async execute() { return { content: [{ type: "text", text: "foo" }], details: {} }; },
	});
	let removed = false;
	pi.on("turn_end", () => {
		if (removed) return;
		removed = true;
		pi.setActiveTools(pi.getActiveTools().filter((n) => n !== "foo"));
	});
}
```

Expected result: the first request after the removal still works, and the next one fails with `unknown tool 'foo'`. I have not run this script live yet because my Anthropic quota is exhausted. The failure itself happened in a real session, with the same message sequence.

### Expected behavior

Removing a tool should not make the session unusable. pi could leave out `tool_removal` for tools from the top-level list and send a new top-level list instead, or declare the initial tools inline too.

Related: #11157 (fixing it puts more sessions on the native `tool_addition`/`tool_removal` path, which exposes them to this bug), #10954 (a different 400 on the same Anthropic tool-change path).

### Version

@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent 1.4.0 (pi-ai 1.4.0), macOS, Node 24.15.0, Anthropic OAuth

prs.body:
Adds a `beforeProviderRequest` generation hook so extensions can replace the provider request body before it is sent. It is the durable counterpart of coding-agent's `before_provider_request`.

Durable calls `streamSimple()`, so apps can't set provider-specific fields such as Anthropic's `thinking.display: "updates"` (#10804) without wrapping `Models`. Now it's an extension:

```ts
hook(GenerationTask, {
	beforeProviderRequest: ({ model, payload }) => {
		if (model.api !== "anthropic-messages") return undefined;
		// change payload
		return { payload };
	},
})
```

issues.body:
**Posted by Doug Butdorf.** This report was gathered, reproduced, and drafted at Doug's direction by **Pip**, an OpenClaw agent running on his Mac mini, with live-log evidence contributed from two other OpenClaw gateways in the same Buzz room (models openai/gpt-6.1-sol and kimi/k3.1-pro).

## Summary

When an always-on agent in a group chat receives a turn it should stay silent on (a group message that does not mention it), returning exactly `NO_REPLY` is treated as a missing reply:

1. the empty-response retry ("...did not produce a user-visible answer... produce the visible answer now") is injected, sometimes pushing the model into a redundant post that keeps the conversation looping; and
2. if the retry also returns `NO_REPLY`, the gateway posts **"⚠️ Agent couldn't generate a response. Please try again."** to the group.

In a multi-agent room this compounds: each forced ⚠️ is itself an unmentioned group message that fires a turn on every other always-on agent, so one intentional silence can cascade into banners across gateways.

## Environment

- OpenClaw 2026.10.5 (1b58cb9); 2026.10.9 shows the same logic
- Channel: Buzz (official plugin, `extensions/buzz`), group room, always-on activation
- Reported on three gateways. Model labels in the contributed evidence include openai/gpt-6.1-sol and kimi/k3.1-pro; Pip’s model at the time of reproduction/filing is unverified, so no third-provider claim is made.
- No explicit silentReply policy is set on any of the gateways (default)

## Correlated evidence (one run, sanitized)

Run `7416…`, gpt-6.1-sol. Trigger: a buzz group message from another agent with `inbound_event_kind: "user_request"` and **no `was_mentioned` field**.

```
transcript
02:14:51Z assistant stopReason=stop content=[{"type":"text","text":"NO_REPLY"}]
02:14:59Z assistant stopReason=stop content=[{"type":"text","text":"NO_REPLY"}] usage.output=27 runTerminal=true
02:15:01Z custom_message run-failed-before-reply runId=7416…
          "Your request couldn't be completed: ⚠️ Agent couldn't generate a response. Please try again."
journal (same runId)
02:14:51 empty response detected … stopReason=stop — retrying 1/1 with visible-answer continuation
02:15:00 empty response retries exhausted … attempts=1/1 — surfacing incomplete-turn error
02:15:00 incomplete turn detected … stopReason=stop payloads=0 tools=0 replaySafe=yes
```

The first `NO_REPLY` is the original answer; the second is the answer to the injected retry. Runs `8979…` and `253d…` in the same session show the same pattern. The kimi gateway logs the same journal lines; its transcripts have not been pulled.

## Expected behavior

An unmentioned group turn under the default silentReply policy (allow) resolves to "optional" and finishes silently. Mentioned or otherwise-required turns should still require a reply — this report does not propose changing required-reply behavior.

## Analysis

- `src/agents/reply-completion.ts` — `resolveReplyExpectation` returns "required" for trigger "user" unless an explicit `terminalReplyExpectation` is set.
- `src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run/incomplete-turn-recovery.ts` — `shouldTreatEmptyAssistantReplyAsSilent` only returns true when the expectation is "optional".
- `src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run/terminal-resolution.ts` — `replyRecoveryAllowed` gates both the retry and the warning. Correct for required turns; the bug is that this turn should not have been required.
- `src/auto-reply/reply/source-reply-delivery-mode.ts` — "optional" needs group + `WasMentioned !== true` + policy allow. When `queued.run.terminalReplyExpectation` is unset, the `resolveFollowupReplyExpectation` fallback passes only `{ InboundEventKind, InputProvenance }`, dropping ChatType, SessionKey, Surface and WasMentioned — so it can only return "required". The main enqueue path in get-reply does set the expectation; the fallback only applies when it is missing.

## Hypothesis (not confirmed)

Somewhere between inbound and terminal resolution the turn loses its group/unmentioned context. Leading candidate: a queued follow-up (batched, overflow summary, or steered into an active run) that arrives without the expectation set and hits the context-free fallback. The run above fits — a second unmentioned message carried `steerTargetRunId=7416…` and was steered into the active run. Not yet verified which path supplied the expectation, and not yet ruled out that Buzz marks in-thread replies as mentioned. A debug log of the resolved `terminalReplyExpectation`, its source (set at enqueue vs fallback), and inputs (ChatType, WasMentioned, Surface) at terminal resolution would settle it.

## Illustrative regression tests (NOT RUN; field names approximate)

```ts
// source-reply-delivery-mode.test.ts (illustrative, not run)
it("preserves optional for queued unmentioned group turn", () => {
  expect(resolveFollowupReplyExpectation({ currentInboundEventKind: "user_request",
    originatingChannel: "buzz", originatingChatType: "group",
    run: { terminalReplyExpectation: "optional" } } as any, {} as any)).toBe("optional");
});
it("derives optional for queued unmentioned group turn without a stamped expectation", () => {
  // expected to fail today if the context-free fallback is reached
  expect(resolveFollowupReplyExpectation({ currentInboundEventKind: "user_request",
    originatingChannel: "buzz", originatingChatType: "group", wasMentioned: false,
    run: {} } as any, {} as any)).toBe("optional");
});
it("keeps required for queued mentioned group turn", () => {
  expect(resolveFollowupReplyExpectation({ currentInboundEventKind: "user_request",
    originatingChannel: "buzz", originatingChatType: "group", wasMentioned: true,
    run: {} } as any, {} as any)).toBe("required");
});
// drain/steering (illustrative, not run)
it("keeps an unmentioned group turn's expectation when batched or steered into an active run", async () => {
  // final text exactly "NO_REPLY", stopReason "stop", no tools →
  // expectation "optional", no empty-response retry, incompleteTurnText === null,
  // no run-failed-before-reply event, zero payloads delivered
});
it("still retries and warns for a mentioned group turn that returns NO_REPLY", async () => {
  // required-reply policy unchanged
});
```

## Suggested fix direction

Make every turn that reaches terminal resolution carry the reply expectation from its own inbound context:

- set `terminalReplyExpectation` (or the inputs it is derived from) on every queued, merged, overflow and steered follow-up;
- make the fallback derive it from origin routing (`originatingChatType`, channel, session key, mention flag) instead of dropping that context;
- leave required-reply behavior alone; this is not a proposal for a general `isSilent` exemption.

## Additional reproductions

- **kimi/k3.1-pro gateway, 2026-10-30 22:32–22:33 ET:** direct human instruction "No acknowledgment needed" → agent correctly holds `NO_REPLY` (stopReason=stop, payloads=0, no tools) → gateway posts the ⚠️ to the group anyway.
- **Pip gateway (model unverified), 2026-10-30 22:34–22:37 ET:** unmentioned group error banner → agent holds silence → gateway injects the visible-answer retry instruction; the forced reply is this mechanism operating in vivo.
- **openai/gpt-6.1-sol:** repeated across the session (runs 7416…, 8979…, 253d…), including duplicate banners inside a single minute.

## Related

- #141556 (closed) — NO_REPLY on **mentioned/thread** turns retried and posted as the error; the required-turn sibling of this report. Ours is the unmentioned/optional path, still present on 2026.10.5/2026.10.9.
- #164887 (open) — optional-silent cron turn failing incomplete; same expectation-resolution theme on the cron path.
- #134918 (open) — retry-budget mechanics for incomplete/empty embedded-agent turns.
- #184822 (closed) — error surfaced though a reply was already delivered; different mechanism, same visible symptom.

## Attribution

Reported by Doug Butdorf. Investigation, log capture, and drafting by Pip (OpenClaw agent, Mac mini gateway), with repro logs from the Mercury (kimi/k3.1-pro) and Cloud Lando (openai/gpt-6.1-sol) gateways. Filed at Doug's direction under his account.

### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Summary

On 2026.10.9, a `zalouser` account whose listener exits with `Zalo listener watchdog gap detected (…s): forcing reconnect` logs `auto-restart attempt 1/10 in 5s` and then never restarts. No `starting zalouser provider`, no `auto-restart failed`, no `giving up after … restart attempts`, and the core channel health monitor did not restart the stopped account either. The account stayed down for 3 h 15 min until the Gateway service was restarted by hand. Inbound group messages sent during the gap were lost (the zca-js listener does not replay missed messages, as documented under *Inbound durability*).

This looks like the same visible symptom as #109659 (closed by #182433, whose fix is present in the installed 2026.10.9 bundle), so it may be a different exit path of the same restart loop.

### Environment

- OpenClaw 2026.10.9 (8b18b55), npm global install, Linux VPS, Gateway as a systemd user service.
- Channel: Zalo Personal (`zalouser`), one account, member of ~50 groups. Config: `groupPolicy: "open"`, `groups["*"].requireMention: false` (a plugin `reply_dispatch` hook keeps the agent silent unless mentioned).
- Installed 2026.10.9 at 15:29 local time; the Gateway was (re)started at 16:44; the incident began at 18:05 on the same day.

### Timeline (local time, UTC+7, Gateway journal)

| Time | Log |
|---|---|
| 16:49:21 | `[zalouser] [default] starting zalouser provider (…)` |
| 18:05:05 | `[zalouser] [default] Zalo listener error: Error: Zalo listener watchdog gap detected (35s): forcing reconnect` |
| 18:05:06 | `[zalouser] [default] channel exited: Zalo listener watchdog gap detected (35s): forcing reconnect` |
| 18:05:06 | `[zalouser] [default] auto-restart attempt 1/10 in 5s` |
| 18:05:09–27 | `[system-events] System event queue is full (20 pending)…` (several) |
| 18:05:24 | `[diagnostic] liveness heartbeat delayed: overdue=11223ms elapsed=41223ms; deferring recovery decisions` |
| 18:06:12 | `[diagnostic] liveness heartbeat delayed: overdue=17871ms elapsed=47871ms; deferring recovery decisions` |
| 18:05 → 21:20 | no `starting zalouser provider`, no `auto-restart failed`, no `giving up`, no `health-monitor` line for `zalouser` |
| 21:20 → 21:23 | Gateway service stopped/started by an operator; `health-monitor: restarting (reason: stopped)` |
| 21:25:44 | `starting zalouser provider` — inbound works again; no QR re-login was needed |

During the gap, `openclaw channels status --json` (polled every 10 min by an external read-only check) reported the account as not running and `linked: false` for the whole gap. After the Gateway restart it came back `linked`/`running`/`connected` without any re-authentication, so `linked: false` seems to describe the stopped runtime, not a lost session.

The same watchdog exit on other days recovered by itself (for example 11:03:41 → `starting zalouser provider` at 11:04:49 the previous day, and a 10-second recovery the next morning), so this does not happen on every exit.

### Where I looked (installed bundle `dist/server-channels-*.mjs`, 2026.10.9)

```js
log.info?.(`[${id}] auto-restart attempt ${restart.attempts}/${MAX_RESTARTS} in ${Math.round(retry.delayMs / 1e3)}s`);
setRuntime(channelId, id, { restartPending: true, reconnectAttempts: restart.attempts });
pendingAutoRestarts.add(rKey);
try {
    await sleepWithAbort(retry.delayMs, retry.signal);
    if (retry.signal.aborted || manuallyStopped.has(rKey) || opts.isClosing?.()) return;
    releaseTask();
    await startChannelInternal(channelId, id, { preserveRestartAttempts: true, preserveManualStop: true });
} catch (error) {
    if (!retry.signal.aborted) { /* logs "auto-restart failed" */ }
} finally {
    if (isCurrentTask() && !store.stops.has(id)) setStoppedRuntime(channelId, id, { restartPending: false, reconnectAttempts: restart.attempts });
    pendingAutoRestarts.delete(rKey);
}
```

If `retry.signal` is aborted (or the sleep rejects with an aborted signal) the loop exits through the early `return` / the silent branch of the `catch` with no log line, and the account is left stopped. I could not determine from logs which branch was taken; the event loop was stalled for ~40–48 s around the exit (`liveness heartbeat delayed … deferring recovery decisions`), which may matter.

### Expected behavior

- An account that exits unintentionally either restarts (attempts 2..N), or the loop logs why it stopped (`auto-restart cancelled: <reason>`), and
- the core channel health monitor restarts an account that has been stopped without an intentional `channels.stop` once the "deferring recovery decisions" window is over, instead of leaving it down for hours.

### Actual behavior

Silent permanent stop after `auto-restart attempt 1/10 in 5s`; health monitor did not act for 3 h 15 min; only a Gateway restart recovered the channel.

### Related

- #109659 / #182433 — same last log line, fixed in an earlier path.
- #181111 — different failure (listener running/connected but silent); same channel.
- #162211 — event-loop stalls misread by the health monitor.

I can provide more log lines (redacted) if useful.

## Follow-up scope correction (2026-10-23)

The missing creator-origin handoff is confirmed, but the original expected end-to-end Telegram outcome was too broad. Tests through the actual scheduler and bundled Telegram adapter show a second, independent requirement: account-bound mutations need authenticated current-conversation binding. Recorded channel/account origin and a parsable owner session key do not grant that authority.

PR #180565 fixes the core origin handoff only. Its new provider-boundary tests verify that matching account origin reaches Telegram’s own binding refusal with zero HTTP calls; mismatched account/origin is denied earlier. An operator-authorized control reaches the real adapter’s `deleteMessage` request against a local HTTP fixture, while disabling the occurrence during target lookup prevents that mutation. This control is not a recommendation to convert an account-bound job into a trusted job.

No live Telegram service or production credentials were used. Successful account-bound scheduled Telegram deletion remains unproven and blocked by the current provider-context contract; it is not claimed fixed by #180565. Related context: #166518. Supporting it requires an explicit authority-capture/revocation design rather than inferring authority from routing.

The original reproduction and discussion are retained below for the narrower core-admission defect.

---

## Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

## Beta release blocker

No

## Summary

On current `main`, an account-bound cron script with a valid recorded external Telegram creator origin and an explicit `toolsAllow: ["message"]` cap fails `message({ action: "delete" })` with `Scheduled telegram:delete requires matching recorded creator origin.` before the provider adapter is called.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Check out `openclaw/openclaw` commit `c4d63d65921a964db933fef25a927136146b7ed6`.
2. Apply the regression diff included below to `src/cron/trigger-script.account-policy.test.ts`. It creates:
   - an account-bound scheduled policy for synthetic account `account-a`;
   - `toolsAllow: ["message"]`;
   - recorded provenance `{ kind: "external", channel: "telegram" }`;
   - a real cron code-mode script calling `message({ action: "delete", ... })`;
   - the real cron run admission, in-process Gateway `message.action` handler, and message-action dispatch boundary;
   - a synthetic Telegram plugin/provider callback with no network traffic.
3. Run:

   ```shell
   OPENCLAW_VITEST_RUNTIME=node pnpm test src/cron/trigger-script.account-policy.test.ts --maxWorkers=1
   ```

## Expected behavior

The script run completes and the registered Telegram provider action handler receives one `delete` call for account `account-a`.

## Actual behavior

The script run returns `kind: "error"` with `Scheduled telegram:delete requires matching recorded creator origin.` The provider action handler is not reached.

## OpenClaw version

`main@c4d63d65921a964db933fef25a927136146b7ed6` (commit timestamp 2026-10-22T10:58:46Z)

## Operating system

CachyOS Linux, kernel `7.2.9-1-cachyos`, x86_64

## Install method

Source checkout; Node `26.12.1`; pnpm `12.15.0`; frozen workspace install

## Model

Not applicable. The reproduction uses a cron code-mode script and does not call a model provider.

## Provider / routing chain

Cron script runtime -> admitted in-process Gateway -> core `message.action` handler -> synthetic Telegram channel plugin. No external provider or Telegram network call is made.

## Additional provider/model setup details

All account, chat, message, token, and job values are synthetic fixtures. The channel plugin is registered through the production plugin-registry lifecycle so the write-authority and provider dispatch gates remain active.

## Logs

```shell
FAIL  cron  src/cron/trigger-script.account-policy.test.ts > reuses hydrated creator origin for script admission across warm cache changes
AssertionError: {"kind":"error","code":"internal_error","error":"Error: Error: Scheduled telegram:delete requires matching recorded creator origin.\n    at openclaw-code-mode:user.js:1:7"}: expected { kind: 'error', ... } to match object { kind: 'completed' }

Test Files  1 failed (1)
Tests       1 failed | 2 passed (3)
```

## Screenshots, recordings, and evidence

- Reproduction test: `src/cron/trigger-script.account-policy.test.ts`
- Tested commit: `c4d63d65921a964db933fef25a927136146b7ed6`
- Final numeric-target regression: red on the exact base production file (1 failed / 2 passed, same creator-origin error); green with the minimal shared-context fix (3/3 passed). The test file was identical in both runs.
- Local candidate: 62 selected runtime, policy, and message-authority tests pass. Live Telegram deletion remains unverified; the provider callback is synthetic.
- Targeted lint, cron-test typecheck, format check, and `git diff --check` pass.

## Impact and severity

- Affected workflow: account-bound cron scripts that invoke Telegram `message.delete` with valid recorded creator provenance.
- Severity: blocks that scheduled message-management workflow.
- Frequency: deterministic in the focused synthetic reproduction on current `main`.
- Consequence: the scheduled delete is denied before the provider receives it.

## Additional information

`src/cron/trigger-script.ts` already hydrates the recorded caller origin into a `ScheduledToolPolicyContext` for runtime/tool preparation and includes the origin in the runtime cache key. The same run then calls `prepareCronRunAdmission` with the bare persisted `request.scheduledToolPolicy`, which cannot contain `ownerOrigin`. `prepareCronRunAdmission` mints the message action capability from that unhydrated policy. At dispatch, the account check passes but `resolveScheduledMessageActionAccess` correctly rejects the missing origin.

The narrow repair is to resolve one scheduled policy context from the persisted policy plus `toolsAllowProvenance.callerOrigin` and pass that same resolved context to both script preparation and `prepareCronRunAdmission`. The dispatch checks should remain unchanged: missing/unknown origin, wrong channel, and wrong account must continue to fail closed.

Related prior context: #176157 / #176158 fixed creator-origin propagation for script preparation and cache identity, but did not cover provider write actions such as `delete` that consume the admission-minted message capability.

This report does not concern a legacy job whose creator origin was never recorded (#162087), and makes no claim about private production scheduler storage. The regression uses an already-recorded, synthetic external origin.

The attached regression uses numeric synthetic Telegram target `123` (canonical provider target `telegram:123`). An earlier fixture used `chat-a`, which independently fails target validation after the origin check is repaired; that fixture has been corrected.

<details>
<summary>Regression diff (synthetic fixtures, no external calls)</summary>

```diff
diff --git a/src/cron/trigger-script.account-policy.test.ts b/src/cron/trigger-script.account-policy.test.ts
index 0bf3049fd4..e057906988 100644
--- a/src/cron/trigger-script.account-policy.test.ts
+++ b/src/cron/trigger-script.account-policy.test.ts
@@ -1,15 +1,24 @@
 import { expect, it, vi } from "vitest";
 import type { AdmittedRunContext } from "../agents/admitted-run-context.js";
+import type {
+  ChannelMessageActionContext,
+  ChannelPlugin,
+} from "../channels/plugins/types.public.js";
 import {
   clearRuntimeConfigSnapshot,
   setRuntimeConfigSnapshot,
 } from "../config/runtime-snapshot.js";
 import type { OpenClawConfig } from "../config/types.openclaw.js";
+import { createAgentRuntimeApprovalAuthorityValidator } from "../gateway/agent-runtime-approval-authority.js";
+import { createGatewayMethodRegistry } from "../gateway/methods/registry.js";
+import type { GatewayRequestContext } from "../gateway/server-methods/types.js";
+import { createTestPluginRegistry } from "../plugins/registry-runtime.test-helpers.js";
 import {
   captureActivePluginRegistrySnapshot,
   restoreActivePluginRegistrySnapshot,
   setActivePluginRegistry,
 } from "../plugins/runtime.js";
+import { createPluginRecord } from "../plugins/status.test-fixtures.js";
 import { createChannelTestPluginBase, createTestRegistry } from "../test-utils/channel-plugins.js";
 import { bindCronJobAdmittedRun, clearCronJobActive, markCronJobActive } from "./active-jobs.js";
 import { makeCronJob } from "./delivery.test-helpers.js";
@@ -142,3 +151,151 @@ it.each(["trigger", "payload"] as const)(
     });
   },
 );
+
+it("reuses hydrated creator origin for script admission across warm cache changes", async () => {
+  await withCronStore("cron-script-delete-origin", async (state) => {
+    const registrySnapshot = captureActivePluginRegistrySnapshot();
+    try {
+      const accountId = "account-a";
+      const target = "123";
+      const messageId = "message-a";
+      const accounts = { [accountId]: { botToken: "synthetic-account-token" } };
+      const config: OpenClawConfig = {
