## What Problem This Solves

Visitor Access used an unbounded JSON read for Cloudflare policy responses. An unexpectedly large response could be buffered before the plugin validated it.

## Why This Change Was Made

Cloudflare policy responses now use the shared bounded provider JSON reader before schema validation. The existing safe error handling remains in place for malformed or oversized responses. GitHub account resolution continues through the Gateway resolver.

## User Impact

Normal Cloudflare policy responses continue to work. Oversized responses fail safely, and the client closes the response stream before receiving the full body.

## Evidence

- On exact rebased HEAD `85f5ea1857a64a082ee63616f0374975580bf0b6`, the focused Visitor Access and Cloudflare suites passed 72 tests (Vitest duration 1.80 s; validation command wall time 18.28 s).
- The last recorded [GitHub CI run](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/37639895695) was for the pre-rebase head `2149ac2793957cc0abd8e03efe0f2dcedb4d590f`; it passed 26 jobs in 14 min 24 s, with `check-test-types` taking 8 min 59 s (2026-10-07 14:49:04–15:03:28 UTC).
- Exact-head production proof ran `proof-live.mjs` with Vitest's installed TSX runner. It imported the production `VisitorPolicyClient`; only the external Cloudflare endpoint was replaced by a local loopback HTTP fixture.
- A valid 1 MiB JSON response was accepted. A streaming response with 32 MiB of padding was rejected with the production safe error. The loopback server observed its response close before completion after writing 17,825,831 of 33,554,432 padding bytes. An HTTP 503 negative control returned the expected request error.

