## What Problem This Solves

When Copilot's usage endpoint returns malformed JSON with HTTP 200, the usage report shows an internal bounded-reader diagnostic instead of useful recovery guidance.

## Why This Change Was Made

The Copilot plugin normalizes only its existing labeled JSON parse and decode failures. The shared bounded reader keeps its 16 MiB limit, stream cancellation, fatal UTF-8 decoding, and transport-failure behavior. This changes no shared SDK contract and does not recover quota from malformed data; whether a wording-only diagnostic change is worth keeping remains a maintainer decision.

## User Impact

The usage report now tells operators that Copilot returned unreadable data and to try refreshing usage later. Healthy providers and valid Copilot quotas remain visible.

## Evidence

- Validation base: `2cbc6abdc23677cd3ff9c874b5e15887069c6602`; exact tested HEAD: `c4ba6cc7ddc9e07e5acae1caa5a4138b95a27415`.
- The same production status CLI and loopback HTTP fixture ran on the validation base and exact HEAD with an HTTP 200 body of `{`. The base printed the reader diagnostic; the exact HEAD printed the recovery guidance. The healthy Claude provider stayed visible in both runs.
- The exact-head valid-response negative control kept Copilot's Premium and Chat quotas visible. The harness redirected only allowlisted GitHub and Anthropic HTTP requests to localhost, used disposable auth/state, and made no external provider call.
